  "id": "Compound::DB00641 - Gene::6579",
  "verbalization": "Simvastatin, Lovastatin, and Pravastatin are all statin drugs that inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, a key enzyme in cholesterol synthesis. This shared mechanism of action places them in the same class and suggests a common pathway of interaction. The evidence indicates that these drugs cause specific physiological effects, such as Menarche or Facial Paresis, which are mediated through Lovastatin or Pravastatin, respectively, and ultimately through their binding to Slco1a2. The lowest common ancestors of Simvastatin and its metabolites, such as Lovastatin and Pravastatin, point to HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors and hexahydronaphthalenes, reinforcing the central role of this class of drugs in the observed biological outcomes. The binding of these drugs to Slco1a2 appears to be a critical step in the cascade of events leading to the physiological effects, though the exact functional significance of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
